        <title>KKM meets colorful Carathéodory</title>
            <categories>math.CO math.HO</categories>
            <comments>6 pages</comments>
            <license>http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</license>
            <abstract>The colorful Carathéodory theorem states that if $V_1,\dots,V_{d+1}\subset\mathbb R^d$ satisfy $0\in\mathrm{conv}\;V_i$ for every $i$, then there exist points $v_i\in V_i$ such that $0\in\mathrm{conv}\;\{v_1,\dots,v_{d+1}\}$.
  We give a new proof based on triangulations and the classical KKM lemma. We lift the sets $V_i$ to parallel affine copies of $\mathbb R^d$ in $\mathbb R^d\times\mathbb R^d$ and use a triangulation of the resulting point configuration to construct a KKM cover. Using the same approach, we prove the very colorful Carathéodory theorem.</abstract>
    </arXiv>

            </metadata>
